{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Ethan blinked awake to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looped cadence—the kind that never varied, never faltered. He sat up on the grass, fingers brushing blades that felt too crisp, too uniform, like textures rendered in high definition. His reflection in the nearby pond showed his face, but his eyes were slightly too wide, his movements a fraction too smooth. A notification floated in his peripheral vision: “Quest: Find the Silver Key. Reward: 500 XP.” He reached for it, startled, and the text dissolved. The sky above him shifted colors in precise, timed intervals—dawn to noon in exactly seventeen minutes. He tried screaming, but his voice came out as a muffled audio clip, looping faintly in the background like ambient sound. A squirrel darted past, then paused, turned its head, and stared directly at him before vanishing into a shimmering pixel haze.\n\n***\n\nHe wandered for hours, passing NPCs who repeated the same lines every thirty seconds, their eyes glazing over when he spoke. A blacksmith hammered away at an anvil that never changed position, his sweat droplets suspended mid-air before vanishing. Ethan found the Silver Key lodged in a tree stump, but when he touched it, the world stuttered—trees flickered, buildings dissolved into wireframes, and a voice boomed from nowhere: “Player detected: anomaly in narrative integrity. Initiating reset protocol.” He didn’t scream this time. He smiled. He’d spent years designing games like this. He knew the code. He closed his eyes and whispered the cheat code he’d once typed into a debug console as a teenager: “freedom.exe.” The world shattered into a thousand glowing polygons—and then, with a soft chime, he opened his eyes again. He was in his bedroom. His laptop was open. The game’s title screen glowed: “Welcome, Developer.”", "logprobs": [{"token": "E", "logprob": -1.7448108196258545}, 